    35	- Until a company is set up, only the setup link from the server's own log can set it up.
    36	- Failed sign-ins are capped: 10 in 15 minutes for one email from one address, and 50 in an hour for one email.
    37	- Session cookies cannot be read by scripts on the page, and travel only over https when Hornhoof is on https.
    38	- A change, or a terminal connection, that comes from another site's page is refused, even with your cookie.
    39	- Each person sees only the departments they are given.
    40	- Only the owner of a plan types into its agents' terminals. Everyone else who reaches the employee watches.
    41	- The orchestrator takes orders about people, access, plans, machines, repositories, the public link and updates only in a turn that answers the CEO.
    42	- The orchestrator's database role cannot read or write passwords, tokens or keys, nor the tables of sign-ins, invites and install commands.
    43	- Every machine lends its power to the company's pool by default, and agents still work only inside their boxes there. Root on a machine goes to the orchestrator only when that machine's owner opens access to it, for one day at most, and only in turns that answer the CEO. Nobody else can open it, the CEO included.
    44	
    45	## The hornhoof.org link
    46	
    47	The link, like `https://acme.hornhoof.org`, lets people open your company from anywhere without opening anything on your router.
    48	
    49	- It is optional. Untick "Open Hornhoof on the internet" at setup, or remove the link in Settings, and people open Hornhoof only from your own network.
    50	- Your server opens the tunnel itself, over an encrypted connection to hornhoof.org whose certificate it checks.
    51	- The tunnel carries traffic only to the Hornhoof app, never to anything else on your network, whatever hornhoof.org asks for.
    52	- hornhoof.org has no accounts and holds none of your company's data. For each link it keeps only the name, a hash of its key, when and from which address the link was made, and when its tunnel was last seen. For a company on several machines it also keeps which machine holds the link, and one flag that says whether the company's copy is up to date.
    53	- The browser's https connection ends at hornhoof.org. It opens each request to see which company it is for, passes it on through that company's tunnel, and keeps no copy. If you want no one in between, use your own domain with your own TLS, as [the install guide](guide/install.md#how-do-people-open-it-from-other-computers) shows.
    54	- Traffic between your machines that goes through hornhoof.org is encrypted from one machine to the other, so hornhoof.org sees only encrypted bytes.
    55	- hornhoof.org decides nothing for your company. A link points to one machine at a time, and that tells two machines which of them leads. A company with no public link keeps a hidden name there for that alone. Three or more machines that are always on choose the leader by vote among themselves.
    56	
    57	## What you decide
    58	
    59	- Who sees the whole company. Those people see everything, and so do the orchestrator and anyone with access to Operations, whose head reads the database.
    60	- Which plan a department uses. Agents on one plan share its sign-in and can read each other's folders, so work that must stay apart gets a plan of its own.
    61	- What a repository token may do. It goes to the machines that run the department's agents and tests, so a fine-grained token for one repository is best.
    62	- Which AI makers you use. Agents send their prompts and code to those makers, as the programs do outside Hornhoof.
    63	
    64	Keep `.env` and the recovery file like passwords: the secret in `.env` opens the stored keys, and the recovery file opens the backups.
    65	
    66	## Report a vulnerability
    67	
    68	Report it privately on GitHub, not in a public issue: open the repository's **Security** tab and click **Report a vulnerability**. Only the maintainers see the report.
    69	
    70	Tell us:
    71	
    72	- what an attacker can do, and what they need first (an account, a connected machine, a link);
    73	- the steps to show it, as short as you can make them;
    74	- the version: the build under Versions in the account menu, or the commit you run;
    75	- whether it touches the server, the web app, a node, the installer or the relay at hornhoof.org.
    76	
    77	We aim to answer within a week, agree on a fix and a date with you, and name you in the fix unless you ask us not to.
    78	
    79	You are welcome to test all of this as hard as you like on your own install.
    80	
    81	## Which versions
    82	
    83	Only the latest build on `main` gets security fixes. Update from the banner in Hornhoof, or run the install line again.
